::GEO_U06_L03_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.10
Question:
A coordinate rectangle has base 9 and height 4. What is its area?
{
=A. 36 square units#Rectangle area is base times height, so 9(4)=36.
~B. 26 units#This is related to perimeter, not area.
~C. 13 square units#This adds the dimensions instead of multiplying.
~D. 72 square units#This doubles the correct area.
}


::GEO_U06_L03_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.13
Question:
Which formula finds the area of a triangle?
{
~A. bh#This is the rectangle area formula.
=B. 1/2bh#Triangle area is one-half the product of base and height.
~C. 2b + 2h#This is a perimeter-style expression.
~D. b + h#Adding dimensions does not find triangle area.
}


::GEO_U06_L03_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.10
Question:
Which coordinate strategy can find area when a polygon is not a rectangle?
{
~A. Use only slope#Slope gives direction, not enclosed area.
~B. Use only perimeter#Perimeter does not determine area.
=C. Decompose into familiar shapes#Area can be found by adding areas of decomposed rectangles and triangles.
~D. Count the number of labels#Labels do not determine area.
}


::GEO_U06_L03_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.13
Question:
A floor tile covers 2 ft by 3 ft. What area does one tile cover?
{
~A. 5 ft#This adds dimensions and uses linear units.
~B. 10 ft?#This doubles the product without a reason.
~C. 6 ft#The value is 6, but the unit must be square feet.
=D. 6 ft?#Area is length times width with square units.
}


::GEO_U06_L03_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.10
Question:
Which statement correctly compares perimeter and area?
{
=A. Perimeter uses linear units, and area uses square units#Perimeter measures around; area measures inside.
~B. Both perimeter and area use square units#Perimeter uses linear units.
~C. Perimeter measures inside a figure#Area measures inside a figure.
~D. Area is always larger than perimeter#The numeric comparison depends on the figure and units.
}


